  "body": "What happens: The kind check in `why_not_sane` in `crates/tools/src/tool_sanity.rs` ends with `_ => None` (line 158). A `ToolKind` that is added later passes the check with no kind rule.\n\nFound by: The review of PR #2236 (merged, e2ed62d0b), comment 6977230739. This is not a regression. The old `is_sane` on main had `_ => true`.\n\nWhy the test does not catch it: The equivalence test uses a list of 18 kinds that a person wrote by hand. Its copy of the old rules has the same catch-all. A new kind is not in the list, so the test does not run it.\n\nWanted:\n- An exhaustive `match` on `ToolKind`. Each kind without a rule is named explicitly.\n- The compiler refuses a new kind until `why_not_sane` names it.\n- The test kind list comes from one source (for example a `ToolKind::ALL` constant). Or a test asserts the list length against the enum.\n\nDone means:\n- A new enum variant does not compile until `why_not_sane` names it.\n- The verdicts for the 18 kinds do not change.\n- The equivalence test stays green.\n",
